          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em covariant phase space
            of a system in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            (a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
                of all of those
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                which solve the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            %li
              %p
                the space of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+histories"} field histories
            %li
              %p
                the
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+trajectories"} space of trajectories
                of the system.
          %p
            Often one considers a parameterization of this by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+value+data"} initial value data
            of fields on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
            inside
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %em canonical phase space
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+coordinates"}> canonical coordinates
                ”: the value of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
                themselves, on the Cauchy surface
            %li
              %p
                “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+momenta"}> canonical momenta
                ”: essentially the temporal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
                of the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
          %p
            The “covariant” in “covariant phase space” is to indicate that it is obtained without any (necessarily un-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"}> natural
            , hence in physics jargon: non-covariant) choices of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foliation"} foliation
            of spacetime by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surfaces"} Cauchy surfaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            from the “canonical” to the “covariant” phase space.
          %div{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CanonicalAndCovariantPhaseSpace-240124.jpg", :width => "700px"}/
          %p
            For a system described by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
            mechanics, the covariant phase space comes canonically equipped with a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+structure"} presymplectic structure
            %em phase space
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+phase+space"} reduced phase space
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+space"} quotient space
            of the covariant phase space on which the presymplectic structure refines to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic structure
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+manifold"} Poisson strucure
          %p Typically these phase spaces are (locally) naturally parameterized by the suitable boundary conditions which uniquely determine the corresponding history of the physical system. Much of the literature on phase spaces deals with parameterizing these boundary conditions.
          %p
            For instance for a non-relativistic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            propagating on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemannian+manifold"} Riemannian manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            with the usual
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
            and the momentum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi T
                    %mi x
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in T^*_x X
            of the particle at a given time. Correspondingly the space of all solutions and hence the (covariant) phase space of the system may be identified with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotangent+bundle"} cotangent bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi T
                    %mo *
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T^* X
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            (The term “phase” in “phase space” can be related to the phase of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            in this example, see at
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+and+phase+space+in+physics"} phase and phase space in physics
          %p
            However, even
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+phase+spaces"} reduced phase spaces
            are not all cotangent bundles, typically not, for instance, if they are obtained by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+reduction"} symplectic reduction
          %h2#covariant_phase_space Covariant phase space
          %p There are two main routes to the construction of the covariant phase space,
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                (S) via
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+structure"} presymplectic structure
            %li
              %p
                (P) via
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+algebra"} Poisson algebra
                structure.
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#ViaSymplecticStructure (S) Via pre-symplectic structures
          %p
            We describe the canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+structure"} presymplectic structure
            on the covariant phase space of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local action functional
            %em covariant phase space
            is defined as the space of critical points of an action functional or, equivalently, the space of solutions of its
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
            = succeed "." do
              %em shell
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+bicomplex"} variational bicomplex
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E \to X
            be a smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundle"} fiber bundle
            over an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mn ∞
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_\infty E
            the corresponding
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+differential"} de Rham differential
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mn ∞
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j_\infty E
            decomposes,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mo +
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d} = d + \delta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local action functional
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S : \Gamma(E) \to \mathbb{R}
          %p
            is by definition given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian"} Lagrangian
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo :
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mn ∞
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L : \Gamma(j_\infty E) \to \Omega^{n}(X)
          %p as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ↦
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S : \phi \mapsto \int_X L(j_\infty(\phi))
                  \\,.
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variation
            of the action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            can be written in terms of the vertical differential:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi j
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta S (\phi) = \int_X (\delta L)(j_\infty(\phi)) \,,
          %p
            where the first
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            the variational differential (as interpreted in the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "variational+bicomplex#BicomplexDefinition"} bicomplex definition
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta
            is the vertical differential. Thus, for the purposes of variational calculus, we can concentrate purely on the vertical differential of the Lagrangian, which canonically decomposes as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mi L
                  %mo =
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi δ
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo −
                  %mi d
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta L = EL(\phi)\delta\phi - d \theta(\phi) \,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL(\phi) = 0
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi δ
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL(\phi)\delta\phi
            is a degree-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n,1)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            is a degree-
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1,1)
            element in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+bicomplex"} variational bicomplex
            (one variational form degree and, respectively,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            or
            = succeed "-spacetime" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
          #CanonicalThetaDensityInLocalCoordinates.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              On a local
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate"} coordinate
              patch
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msup
                      %mi x
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x^i\}
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
              here is given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi i
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %mi L
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %msubsup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mrow
                            %mo ,
                            %mi i
                          %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi δ
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi a
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\theta(\phi)
                    \=
                    (\iota_{\partial_i} \frac{\delta L}{\delta  \phi^a_{,i}} )
                    \\wedge
                    \\delta \phi^a
                    \\,.
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi kin
                    %mo +
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi pot
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L = L_{kin} + L_{pot}
              is the sum of a standard
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+Lagrangian"} kinetic Lagrangian
              for a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
              and a potential term that only depends on the fields themselves and not on their derivatives, then this is at the same time the canonical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+form"} multisymplectic form
              for the given
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} multisymplectic geometry
              the section
              %em
                %a{:href => "#CanonicalThetaDensityInLocalCoordinates"} Examples – Free field theory
              for more on this relation.
          %p
            The above definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EL
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            in terms of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta L
            yields the following identity upon taking another exterior variational derivative of both sides
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi δ
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi δ
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mi δ
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\delta(\delta L) = 0
                  \= \delta(EL(\phi)) \wedge \delta\phi
                  + d \delta \theta(\phi)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the first term on the right clearly vanishes when pulled back to the shell,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL(\phi)=0
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi j
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^\bullet(j_\infty E)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+current"} presymplectic current
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi θ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega(\phi) = \delta(\theta(\phi))
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi ι
                            %mo *
                          %mi ω
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi d
                          %msup
                            %mi ι
                            %mo *
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi δ
                          %mi θ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msup
                            %mi ι
                            %mo *
                          %mi d
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi δ
                          %mi θ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %msup
                            %mi ι
                            %mo *
                          %mi δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi EL
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi δ
                          %mi ϕ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  d \iota^* \omega(\phi)
                  \&amp;= d \iota^* (\delta \theta(\phi)) \\
                  \&amp;= \iota^* d (\delta \theta(\phi)) \\
                  \&amp;= -\iota^* \delta(EL(\phi)) \wedge \delta\phi \\
                  \&amp;= 0
                  \\, ,
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ι
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒮
                    %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota: \mathcal{S}_L \to \Gamma(E)
            is the embedding of the solutions of the
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ι
                    %mo *
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota^* \delta(EL(\phi)) = 0
            for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi EL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL(\phi)
            are all constant (in fact
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
          %p The variational 1-form on the space of field configurations
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Θ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi in
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Theta = \int_{X|_{in}} \theta(\phi)
          %p
            given by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integration
            over a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+surface"} Cauchy surface
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi in
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X|_{in}
            is the potential for the presymplectic form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo =
                  %mi δ
                  %mi Θ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi in
                  %mi δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi θ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi in
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega = \delta \Theta = \int_{X|_{in}} \delta(\theta(\phi))
                  \= \int_{X|_{in}} \omega(\phi)
          %p
            on the space of field configurations. Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega(\phi)
            is horizontally closed on shell, the the pullback of the presymplectic form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ι
                    %mo *
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota^* \Omega
            is independent of the choice of the surface
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi in
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X|_{in}
            (provided the choice is restricted to a single homology class of surfaces).
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
            %p
              The presymplectic form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ι
                      %mo *
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota^* \Omega
              on the covariant phase space is symplectic iff the linearized
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi EL
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} EL(\phi)=0
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi in
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X|_{in}
