          %p
          #InverseProblem.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong Application to the inverse problem of the calculus of variations
              %br/
            %p
              We discuss the
              %em inverse problem
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+calculus"} variational calculus
            %p
              given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+form"} presymplectic form
              on the locus of solutions of a system of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+differential+equations"} partial differential equations
              = succeed "?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+action+functional"} local action functional
            %p
              (This section follows
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "#BridgesHydonLawson"} Bridges, Hydon &amp; Lawson
            %p
              We use same notation as above. Namely, dependence on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              in local forms really means dependence on finitely many components of the infinite jet
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi j
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j^\infty(\phi)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota
              denotes the embedding of the space of solutions in the space of field configurations. Moreover, we presume to work on a sufficiently small neighborhoods in the space of solutions and field configurations that the Poincaré lemma applies.
            %p
              Consider a system of partial differential equations
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
