          %h2#references References
          %p A a popular introduction to the notion of phase space with some comments on the history of the concept are in
          %ul
            %li#Nolte10
              David Nolte,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The tangled tale of phase space
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://works.bepress.com/ddnolte/2"} web
          %h3#covariant_phase_space_2 Covariant phase space
          %p
            Textbook account with an eye towards
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST-BV+formalism"} BRST-BV formalism
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marc+Henneaux"}> Marc Henneaux
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Teitelboim"} Claudio Teitelboim
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quantization+of+Gauge+Systems"} Quantization of Gauge Systems
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691037691/quantization-of-gauge-systems"} ISBN:9780691037691
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctv10crg0r"} jstor:j.ctv10crg0r
          %p An article reviewing much of the content of the following references:
          %ul
            %li#Khavkine14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Igor+Khavkine"}> Igor Khavkine
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Covariant phase space, constraints, gauge and the Peierls formula
              %strong 29
              (2014) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1402.1282"}> arXiv:1402.1282
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0217751X14300099"} doi:10.1142/s0217751x14300099
          %p Further review:
          %ul
            %li#Vitagliano
              %p
                L. Vitagliano,
                %em Secondary calculus and the covariant phase space
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0809.4164"}> arXiv:0809.4164
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                M. J. Gotay, J. Isenberg, R. Montgomery, J. E. Marsden,
                %em Momentum Maps and Classical Fields, Part I: Covariant Field Theory
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/download?doi=10.1.1.6.6137&rep=rep1&type=pdf"} pdf
          %p
            A discussion in the context of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variational+bicomplex"} variational bicomplex
            with further pointers to the use in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              E. Reyes,
              %em On Covariant Phase Space and the Variational Bicomplex
              , Int. J. Theor. Phys. 43, no 5 (2004) 1267-1286
          %p
            A discussion in the language of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-modules"} D-modules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chiral+Algebras"} Chiral Algebras
            and leading up to the derived covariant phase space by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BRST-BV+formalism"} BRST-BV formalism
            is in section 8.3 of
          %ul
            %li#Paugam
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+Paugam"}> Frédéric Paugam
              ,
              %em Towards the mathematics of quantum field theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.jussieu.fr/~fpaugam/documents/enseignement/master-mathematical-physics-impa-v01-2011.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
          %ul
            %li#GiotopoulosSati23
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grigorios+Giotopoulos"}> Grigorios Giotopoulos
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
              %em
                Field Theory via Higher Geometry I:
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Smooth+Sets+of+Fields"} Smooth Sets of Fields
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.16301"}> arXiv:2312.16301
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            On the relation between covariant phase space methods and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multisymplectic+geometry"} multisymplectic geometry
          %ul
            %li#Helein
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+H%C3%A9lein"}> Frédéric Hélein
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Multisymplectic formalism and the covariant phase
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Variational problems in differential geometry
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1106.2086"} arXiv:1106.2086
            %li#BridgesHydonLawson
              %p
                Thomas J. Bridges, Peter E. Hydon and Jeffrey K. Lawson,
                %em Multisymplectic structures and the variational bicomplex
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1017/S0305004109990259"}> doi
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#ForgerRomero04
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Forger"}> Michael Forger
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sandro+Romero"} Sandro Romero
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Covariant Poisson brackets in geometric field theory
                %strong 256
                (2005) 375-410 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0408008"}> arXiv:math-ph/0408008
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-005-1287-8"} doi:10.1007/s00220-005-1287-8
          %h3#original_articles_on_covariant_phase_space_technology Original articles on covariant phase space technology
          %p In the following we give a commented list of references following the historical development.
          %p
            It seems that there have been two independent lines of development, somewhat independent of each other, but also with some crosspollination along the way. One stream
            %a{:href => "#ViaPoissonStructure"} (P)
            was concerned with the construction of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+structure"} Poisson structure
            on functions of solutions, while the other
            %a{:href => "#ViaSymplecticStructure"} (S)
            was concerned with the construction of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            on the space of solutions. Obviously, the differences between the two kinds of constructions show up precisely in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
            systems.
          %p The earliest papers are those of Peierls and Bergmann-Schiller in the early 50’s. They seem to have been self-motivated. Below, we try to list papers that have established key results or have served as important popularizers together with their main influences from previous works. The constructions of the (S) stream seem to have appeared nearly independently several times over several decades, until Lee-Wald and followups of Ashtekar’s papers became standard references. The (P) stream flowed slowly, but consistently from original idea of Peierls. The papers of Fredenhagen-Dütsch-et-al are a most readable modern formulation.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                R. Peierls,
                %em The commutation laws of relativistic field theory
                (1952) (
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/pss/99080"}> jstor
                )
              %p (P)
              %p Influence: Appears to be self-motivated. The result may have been guessed from the known unequal-time, covariant Poisson brackets between free fields, which involve the causal Green function in an explicit way.
              %p
                Result: The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls+bracket"} Peierls bracket
                on the covariant phase pace of a non-gauge system is defined and equivalence with the Hamiltonian phase space symplectic structure is proved. Gauge systems are treated by example of electrodynamics. The solution to degenerate equations of motion is either adding gauge fixing terms to the action or consider non-degenerate equations of motion satisfied by gauge invariant fields. Fermions are handled by multiplying fermi fields by formal odd parameters.
            %li
              %p
                Bergmann-Schiller,
                %em Classical and quantum field theories in the Lagrangian Formalism
                Phys. Rev. 89 4-16 (1953) (
                %a{:href => "http://prola.aps.org/abstract/PR/v89/i1/p4_1"}> prola
                )
              %p (S)
              %p Influence: Appears to be motivated by Bergmann’s long term and “brute force” analysis of the gauge invariance structure of relativity and structurally similar theories. Another motivation is the possibility of writing divergence-less stress energy tensors as “curls” of “superpotentials”.
              %p Result: The pre-symplectic potential form-current makes an appearance. The definitions of the covariant phase space and the resulting symplectic form on it are obscure (at least to me), but later papers make reference to them.
            %li
              %p
                Bergmann-Goldberg-Janis-Newman,
                %em Canonical Transformations and Commutators in the Lagrangian Formalism
                Phys. Rev. 103, 807–813 (1956) (
                %a{:href => "http://prola.aps.org/abstract/PR/v103/i3/p807_1"}> prola
                )
              %p (P&amp;S)
              %p Influence: Peierls, Bergmann-Schiller
              %p Result: Proof of the equivalence between the Peierls bracket and the bracket obtained by Bergmann-Schiller for non-gauge systems. The treatment of gauge systems and many of the calculations are maybe a bit obscure.
            %li
              %p
                Komar,
                %em Commutators on Characteristic Surfaces
                Phys. Rev. 134, B1430–B1440 (1964) (proceedings w/ Bergmann 1962)
              %p (S)
              %p Influence: Bergmann-Schiller
              %p Result: Uses the methods of Bergmann-Schiller to define Poisson brackets on initial data for linearized gravitational waves on null hypersurfaces, including at null infinity.
            %li
              %p
                DeWitt B.S., DeWitt C. (eds.)
                %em Relativity, Groups, and Topology
                (Les Houches 1963, Gordon and Breach, 1964), pp.585-820 (1964; lecture notes 1963)
              %p (P)
              %p Influence: Peierls
              %p Result: A more detailed exposition of Peierls’ idea, with a bit more generality in the treatment of gauge systems. Becomes a standard reference along with Peierls’ original paper.
            %li
              %p
                Friedman,
                %em Generic instability of rotating relativistic stars
                Commun. math. Phys. 62, 247–278 (1978) (
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01202527"}> springer
                )
              %p (S)
              %p Influence: Bergmann-Schiller, Komar, … (?)
            %li
              %p
                Ashtekar-Magnon,
                %em On the symplectic structure of general relativity
                Communications in Mathematical Physics Volume 86, Number 1, 55-68, DOI: 10.1007/BF01205661 (1982) (
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01205661"}> springer
                )
              %p (S)
              %p Influence: Komar, also some more obscure papers by Fridmann and Palmer
              %p Result: Write down the explicit pre-symplectic current and the corresponding pre-symplectic form on the space of solutions of linearized gravitational wave equations. This symplectic form on the reduced space of solutions is shown to coincide with the ADM symplectic structure and with the Komar symplectic structure at null infinity. The motivation explicit pre-symplectic current formula appears from the desire to interpolate between the above two formalisms.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
                , §5 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Interacting field theory of open superstrings
                %strong 276
                2 (1986) 291-324 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(86)90298-1"}> doi:10.1016/0550-3213(86)90298-1
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/228073"} inspire:228073
              %p (S)
              %p
                Influence: Appears to be self-motivated. Witten needed a covariant way to define Poisson brackets from the action of open string field theory. Acknowledges parallel, independent work of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "#Zuckerman"} Zuckerman
              %p Result: Witten writes down the pre-symplectic form-current “by inspection” for a scalar field and for a Chern-Simons theory, then he explicitly shows that the current is conserved on the space of solutions, and that its integral over a Cauchy surface is variationally closed.
            %li
              %p Ashtekar, Bombelli, Koul, YS Kim, WW Zachary (eds.), The Physics of Phase Space: Nonlinear Dynamics and Chaos, Geometric Quantization, and Wigner Function (p.356) ISBN 0-387-17894-5 (1987; preprint 1986)
              %p (S)
              %p Influence: Ashtekar-Magnon
              %p
                Result: Explicit formula for the symplectic form of full, nonlinear
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
                as an integral of a conserved form-current over an arbitrary Cauchy surface. Discussion of conversion of symmetries into conserved quantities using this form. Some of the aspects related to the construction of such conserved have apparently been treated in earlier works by Friedman, Sachs and possibly some others.
            %li#Zuckerman
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gregg+Zuckerman"}> Gregg Zuckerman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Action Principles and Global Geometry
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/ZuckermanVariation.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
              %p (S)
              %p
                Influence: Appears to be self-motivated. Possible motivation is a more general construction of conserved currents in variational problems, a la Noether. Acknowledges that he reproduces parallel, but applied to specific theories, work of
                %a{:href => "#CrnkovićWitten87"} Crnković &amp; Witten (1987)
                and Ashtekar-Bombelli-Koul.
              %p Result: The construction of the pre-symplectic potential form-current and pre-symplectic form-current are given in essentially modern terms. Not all proofs are given. Little discussion of symplectic reduction.
            %li{:id => "CrnkovićWitten87"}
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cedomir+Crnkovi%C4%87"}> Čedomir Crnković
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Covariant Description of Canonical Formalism in Geometrical Theories
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Three Hundred Years of Gravitation
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.ias.edu/sites/default/files/sns/files/CovariantPaper-1987.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/us/universitypress/subjects/physics/cosmology-relativity-and-gravitation/three-hundred-years-gravitation?format=PB&isbn=9780521379762"} ISBN:9780521379762
              %p (S)
              %p Influence: Witten
              %p Result: Applies the “by inspection” methods of Witten’s earlier paper to write down conserved, variationally-closed pre-symplectic currents for Yang-Mills and general relativity.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cedomir+Crnkovi%C4%87"}> Čedomir Crnković
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Symplectic Geometry of the Covariant Phase Space
                %strong 5
                (1988) 1557-1575 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1088/0264-9381/5/12/008"}> doi:10.1088/0264-9381/5/12/008
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/247290"} inspire:247290
            %li
              %p
                J. Lee,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
                %em Local symmetries and constraints
                (1989) J. Math. Phys. 31, 725 (1990); doi:10.1063/1.528801 (
                %a{:href => "http://jmp.aip.org/resource/1/jmapaq/v31/i3/p725_s1?isAuthorized=no"}> jMP
                )
              %p (S)
              %p Influence: Crnković-Witten, Friedman
              %p Result: Essentially gives the modern formulation as described above. The exposition is quite similar to Zuckerman (though no connection with that work is made), but with complete proofs.
            %li
              %p
                Ashtekar-Bombelli-Reula,
                %em The Covariant Phase Space of Asymptotically Flat Gravitational Fields
                in Mechanics, Analysis and Geometry: 200 Years after Lagrange edited by M. Francaviglia and D.~Holm, North-Holland, Amsterdam, 1990. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.famaf.unc.edu.ar/~reula/Papers/200_Lagrange.pdf"}> web
                )
              %p (S)
              %p Influence: Ashtekar-…
              %p Result: Becomes standard reference. (?)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Donald+Marolf"}> Donald Marolf
                ,
              %p
                %em Poisson Brackets on the Space of Histories
                Annals of Physics Volume 236, Issue 2, December 1994, Pages 374-391
                %br>/
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9308141"}> arXiv:hep-th/9308141
                )
              %p
                %em The generalized Peierls bracket
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9308150"}> arXiv:hep-th/9308150
                )
              %p (1994; preprints 1993, thesis 1992)
              %p (P)
              %p Influence: Peierls, DeWitt; mentions but does not use the material from Lee-Wald, Crnkovic-Witten, Ashtekar
              %p Result: Gives the construction outlined above.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Donald+Marolf"} Donald Marolf
                %em Asymptotic generators of fermionic charges and boundary conditions preserving supersymmetry
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/gr-qc/0611044"}> arXiv
                ) (2007, preprint 2006)
              %p (S)
              %p Influence: Lee-Wald
              %p Result: Extends analysis to classical fermi fields.
            %li
              %p
                Dütsch
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                %em The Master Ward Identity and Generalized Schwinger-Dyson Equation in Classical Field Theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0211242"}> arXiv:hep-th/0211242
                ) (2003),
              %p
                Brennecke-Duetsch,
                %em Removal of violations of the Master Ward Identity in perturbative QFT
                (2008) (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0705.3160"}> arXiv:0705.3160
                ),
              %p
                Rejzner,
                %em Fermionic fields in the functional approach to classical field theory
                (2011) (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1101.5126"}> arXiv:1101.5126
                )
              %p (P)
              %p
                Influence: Peierls, DeWitt, Marolf; also some ideas related to retarded products that go back to Glaser-Lehmann-Zimmermann in the 1950s (more precisely: Glaser, V., Lehmann, H. and Zimmermann, W., “Field Operators and Retarded Functions”
                %em Nuovo Cimen.
                %strong 6
                (1957) 1122)
              %p Result: Modern, elementary treatment of the Peierls bracket, including for classical fermi fields. The discussion is simplified by sticking to non-degenerate (or gauge fixed) actions. The choice of coordinates on the space of field configurations is simplified by considering only vector bundle valued fields.
          %p A different but related approach is
          %ul
            %li#KijowskiTulczyjew05
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jerzy+Kijowski"}> Jerzy Kijowski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wlodzimierz+M.+Tulczyjew"} Wlodzimierz M. Tulczyjew
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Symplectic Framework for Field Theories
              %strong 107
              (1979, 2005) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-09538-1"}> doi:10.1007/3-540-09538-1
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            also discussed (with focus on application to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+S.+Cattaneo"}> Alberto S. Cattaneo
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Phase space for gravity with boundaries
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.04666"} arXiv:2307.04666
          %p
            for the case of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds+with+boundary"} manifolds with boundary
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Harlow"}> Daniel Harlow
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jie-qiang+Wu"} Jie-qiang Wu
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Covariant phase space with boundaries
                %strong 2020
                146 (2020). &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08616"}> arXiv:1906.08616
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/JHEP10(2020)146"} doi:10.1007/JHEP10(2020)146
            %li
              %p
                Juan Margalef-Bentabol, Eduardo J. S. Villaseñor,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometric formulation of the covariant phase space methods with boundaries
                %strong 103
                (2021) 025011 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevD.103.025011"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevD.103.025011
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2008.01842"} arXiv
            %li
              %p
                Valle Varo,
                %em The Covariant Phase Space of Gravity with Boundaries
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.12418"}> arXiv:2301.12418
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alejandro+Corichi"}> Alejandro Corichi
                , Juan D. Reyes, Tatjana Vukasinac,
                %em On covariant and canonical Hamiltonian formalisms for gauge theories
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10229"}> arXiv:2312.10229&amp;rbrack;
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Vinícius Bernardes,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theodore+Erler"} Theodore Erler
              %em
                Covariant phase space and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
                algebras
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.20706"}> arXiv:2506.20706
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#reduced_phase_space Reduced phase space
          %p
            Standard textbooks on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mechanics"} classical mechanics
            include
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Arnold"}> Vladimir Arnold
                ,
                %em Mathematical methods of classical mechanics
            %li
              %p
                H. Goldstein,
                %em Classical mechanics
            %li
              %p
                L. D. Landau, E. M. Lifshitz,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Mechanics
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Landau-Lifschitz"} Landau-Lifschitz
                Course on theoretical physics)
            %li
              %p
                R. Abraham, J. E. Marsden,
                %em Foundations of mechanics
            %li
              %p
                J. E. Marsden, T. Ratiu,
                %em Introduction to mechanics and symmetry
            %li
              %p
                wikipedia:
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phase_space"} phase space
          %h3#bv_formalism BV formalism
          %p
            Discussion via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BV-formalism"} BV-formalism
            includes
          %ul
            %li#CattaneoMnevReshetikhin12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alberto+Cattaneo"}> Alberto Cattaneo
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pavel+Mnev"} Pavel Mnev
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nicolai+Reshetikhin"} Nicolai Reshetikhin
              %em Classical BV theories on manifolds with boundary
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1201.0290"}> arXiv:1201.0290
              )
        .revisedby
          %p
